---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeMorse from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeMorse.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="morse-code-translator">
  <DevEncodeMorse client:load mode="morse" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How Morse timing works</h2>
    <p>
      Morse code is a rhythm built from one unit of time. All other durations are multiples of it:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Element</th><th>Duration</th><th>State</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Dit (dot)</td><td>1 unit</td><td>Tone on</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dah (dash)</td><td>3 units</td><td>Tone on</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gap inside a letter</td><td>1 unit</td><td>Silence</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gap between letters</td><td>3 units</td><td>Silence</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gap between words</td><td>7 units</td><td>Silence</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Speed is defined against the reference word <code>PARIS</code>, which is exactly 50 units
      long. At 20 words per minute, one unit is 60&nbsp;÷&nbsp;(50&nbsp;×&nbsp;20) = 60&nbsp;ms. The
      audio on this page is generated to those exact durations.
    </p>

    <h2>Farnsworth spacing, and why it matters for learning</h2>
    <p>
      Learners usually fail because they count individual dits. That works up to about 10&nbsp;WPM and
      then stops working, because at speed you must recognise the whole rhythm of a letter as one
      shape.
    </p>
    <p>
      Farnsworth timing stops that habit. Characters are sent fast (18 to 20&nbsp;WPM), and the gaps
      between them are longer, so the overall pace is slow enough to follow. With the two sliders, set
      the character speed above the overall speed. You hear each letter at full speed with time to
      think between letters, and you never have to learn the letters again at a higher rate.
    </p>

    <h2>Letters, digits and punctuation</h2>
    <p>
      The full reference grid is in the tool above. Some patterns make it easier to learn.
      <code>E</code> is a single dit and <code>T</code> a single dah, because they are the most common
      letters in English: Morse was an early example of frequency coding. All digits are five elements
      long and follow a clean pattern, from 1 as <code>.----</code> to 0 as <code>-----</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Prosigns</h2>
    <p>
      A prosign is several letters sent as one unbroken character, with no letter gap. So SOS is a
      distress signal and not three letters:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Prosign</th><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SOS</td><td><code>...---...</code></td><td>Distress. Chosen because it is unmistakable, not as an abbreviation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AR</td><td><code>.-.-.</code></td><td>End of message</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SK</td><td><code>...-.-</code></td><td>End of contact</td></tr>
          <tr><td>BT</td><td><code>-...-</code></td><td>Break, new section</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HH</td><td><code>........</code></td><td>Error. Disregard the last word</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The story that SOS stands for &ldquo;save our souls&rdquo; is a backronym. SOS was adopted in 1906
      because the pattern is symmetrical, distinctive and hard to confuse with other signals under
      noise.
    </p>

    <h2>The audio is built here, not fetched</h2>
    <p>
      Playback builds an audio buffer sample by sample with the Web Audio API. A short 5&nbsp;ms ramp at
      the start and end of each tone prevents the click of a sine wave that starts abruptly. The WAV
      download writes the same samples into a 16-bit PCM file with a hand-built header. No audio is
      downloaded, so the Morse code translator works offline.
    </p>

    <h2>Visual flash mode</h2>
    <p>
      Turn on the flash toggle to see the message as light: the same timing, as a panel that lights on
      each tone. Ships signal Morse by lamp this way, and it also makes the tool usable with the sound
      off or with hearing loss.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
